# Let's Encrypt STAGING root certificates.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   Every e2e deploy is forced onto Let's Encrypt's staging ACME server
#   (tests/e2e/utils/e2e-env.js sets ACME_CA_SERVER) because production LE
#   caps at 5 certs / week / identifier and the matrix re-uses the same
#   {e3,e4}.appcarbon.dev names on every run. Staging certificates chain to
#   the roots below, which are deliberately absent from every OS/Node trust
#   store — so the harness has to trust them explicitly.
#
#   Before this file, the runner did `NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED = '0'`,
#   which turned verification OFF for the whole process. That also made a
#   rig serving a self-signed, expired, or wrong-host certificate pass
#   silently — exactly the class of misconfiguration the e2e suite exists to
#   catch. Trusting these four roots (and nothing else) keeps verification ON
#   while still accepting the staging chain.
#
# THESE ARE PUBLIC ROOT CERTIFICATES — public keys only, no private key
# material. They are published by ISRG for exactly this purpose and are safe
# to commit.
#
# SOURCE: https://letsencrypt.org/docs/staging-environment/
#   Retrieved 2026-07-30. The four roots listed as current on that page.
#   NOTE: staging INTERMEDIATES ("(STAGING) Pseudo Plum E5", "(STAGING)
#   Counterfeit Cashew R10", ...) are explicitly documented as subject to
#   change at any time and must never be pinned — only the roots belong here.
#   Servers send the intermediates in the TLS handshake, so trusting the
#   roots is sufficient to build a chain.
#
# REFRESHING: re-download the four PEMs below, replace the blocks, and update
# the pinned SHA-256 fingerprints in tests/unit/e2e/staging-ca-trust.test.ts.
# The test fails loudly if a block is missing, expired, or not self-signed.

